  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Organizing Kanji Data**\n\nI\'m currently focused on structuring the Kanji data. My goal is to create clean, modular files for N5, N4, and N3 Kanji sets, ensuring complete lists for each level. The files will be well-organized and easy to navigate for future use.\n\n\n**Structuring Kanji Data**\n\nI\'ve decided to keep `kanjiData.ts` as the main exporter in `src/data/`, but create individual files: `kanjiN5.ts`, `kanjiN4.ts`, `kanjiN3.ts`, and `kanjiN2N1.ts` for clean organization. This will handle the Onyomi, Kunyomi, stroke counts, radicals, JLPT levels, Portuguese meanings, and example compound words (with Furigana and Portuguese translations) neatly. I am now checking the content of N5 Kanjis.\n\n\n**Compiling N4 Kanji**\n\nI\'m now integrating the N4 Kanji list into the appropriate file, categorizing them as I go. The categories like \"Family & Relations,\" \"School & Learning,\" etc., help me organize them logically within the file. I have already compiled the list of 180 kanji into a well formatted list.\n\n\n**Populating N3 Kanji**\n\nI\'m now populating the N3 Kanji list, carefully categorizing each into their respective groups. I\'ve covered sections on emotions, society, work, and actions. The work is going quite well, and each list has about the right amount of Kanji, though I need to keep checking for possible overlap.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 7
      nanos: 495522360
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
